+---
+id: dl-001-resolvepointer-error-semantics-and-native-ts-execution
+type: decision-log
+title: "resolvePointer error semantics and native TS execution"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Implementing `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` (RFC 6901) raised two choices not fully pinned down
+by the task description: (1) exactly which cases count as "does not resolve to a value" and must
+throw, since RFC 6901 leaves some of this to the implementer; (2) how to run and test TypeScript
+source in a package that ships no build step and no test framework beyond `node --test`.
+
+## Decision
+
+1. **Error semantics.** `resolvePointer` throws in every case where RFC 6901 does not guarantee a
+   value, not only on clear syntax errors:
+   - Pointer syntax: non-empty pointers not starting with `/` throw (invalid pointer).
+   - Array indices: only `0` or a non-zero-leading digit sequence is accepted; `-` (RFC 6901's
+     "nonexistent element after the last array element") and out-of-bounds indices throw, since
+     neither names an existing value.
+   - Object members: a missing key throws.
+   - Indexing into a scalar (string, number, boolean, null) with a remaining token throws.
+   This is stricter than "return undefined on miss," matching the task's explicit requirement that
+   the function throws when the pointer "does not resolve to a value of the document."
+
+2. **Native TypeScript execution.** No build step, `ts-node`, or test framework was added. Node 22.23
+   (this project's runtime) runs `.ts` files directly via its built-in type-stripping, enabled by
+   default, so `node --test` (already the `test` script in `package.json`) executes
+   `test/index.test.ts` straight from source. Relative imports in source/tests use the real `.ts`
+   extension (e.g. `from '../src/index.ts'`), since Node's ESM resolver does not substitute
+   extensions. `typescript` was installed ad hoc (not saved to `package.json`) only to run
+   `tsc --noEmit` as a one-off strict-mode type check.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Follow-on features that build on `resolvePointer` can rely on it throwing rather than returning
+  `undefined`/`null` for any unresolvable pointer; callers that want a non-throwing lookup must wrap
+  it in `try`/`catch`.
+- The test suite only runs on a Node version with type-stripping on by default (22.x here); if the
+  project later needs to run on an older Node, or adds a bundler/build step, this choice should be
+  revisited.
+- `typescript` is not yet a declared dependency; anyone wanting `tsc` type-checking locally must
+  install it themselves until/unless a future change adds it (and a lint/typecheck script) formally.
