+status: approved
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Implement JSON Pointer (RFC 6901). Export `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` from `src/index.ts`,
+resolving a JSON Pointer string against a JSON document. It throws when the pointer is not
+syntactically valid, or when it does not resolve to a value in the document. This is the first of
+several features that build on each other; later ones will use `resolvePointer`.
+
+## What was done
+
+- Added `src/index.ts` exporting `resolvePointer(document, pointer)`:
+  - Empty string resolves to the whole document.
+  - Non-empty pointers must start with `/`; reference tokens are split on `/` and unescaped
+    (`~1` → `/`, then `~0` → `~`, per RFC 6901 §3).
+  - Object members are looked up by exact (unescaped) key; missing members throw.
+  - Array elements are looked up by canonical index (`0` or a non-zero-leading digit sequence);
+    `-`, leading zeroes, and out-of-bounds indices all throw, since none resolve to a value.
+  - Indexing into a scalar (string, number, boolean, null) throws.
+- Added `test/index.test.ts` (`node:test`), covering the RFC 6901 §5 worked example (including every
+  escaped-key case) plus the error paths above. Wrote the tests first, confirmed they failed with no
+  implementation present (module-not-found), then implemented `resolvePointer` until all 10 passed.
+- Documented the function in `README.md` under a new "API" section with a usage example.
+- Ran `npx tsc --noEmit` (TypeScript installed ad hoc, not added as a dependency) to confirm the
+  strict-mode types check cleanly; no linter is configured in this repository yet.
+
+## Notes for follow-on features
+
+- `node --test` runs `.ts` test files directly (Node 22's native type-stripping is on by default);
+  no build step or ts-node is needed. Relative imports must use the real `.ts` extension
+  (e.g. `from '../src/index.ts'`), since Node's ESM resolver does not do extension substitution.
+- No `typescript` devDependency exists in `package.json` yet; `tsc` was only used locally to verify
+  types, not wired into any script.
